    "done_when": "reckon crew dispatch gains --repairs RUN_ID, in reckon/cli.py and in reckon/crew/dispatch.py's plan_dispatch. The named run must be a promoted run in the project's ledger. A run that is unknown, not yet promoted, or from another project is refused before launch, with the reason named. The declaration is recorded on the new run's record as repairs: RUN_ID, beside attempt_kind. reckon/crew/promotion.py treats a record carrying repairs exactly as it treats attempt_kind resume or redispatch: it promotes with impl_move recorded as exempt, naming the repaired run, and needs no --no-impl-change. It reuses the existing corrective-attempt check rather than adding a second one. tests/test_dispatch_declares_the_run_it_repairs.py, in a synthesised repository with a temp ledger and pointer directory, covers four cases: a --repairs dispatch naming a promoted run records the field and its promotion is exempt; naming an unknown run is refused; naming a run that is not yet promoted is refused; and a dispatch without --repairs whose plan impl did not move is still refused, as today. It passes at exit 0, as do tests/test_bookkeeping_follows_the_run.py and tests/test_promotion_requires_impl_move.py. The manifest records base and head arms of those three files, each log header printing its revision and reckon.__file__, in the armed JSON form with FAILED ids per arm; the head arm is run at the committed head. The declared negative control is applied, the exempt case is observed to be refused, and that log's absolute path is in the manifest with an EXIT= line and the FAILED test id. reckon/crew/promotion.py is shared with two concurrent nodes that edit other functions; touch only the corrective-attempt check and the record read it needs.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Let a dispatch declare the promoted run it repairs so its promotion is exempt from the impl move",
